<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-even-and-odd-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Even and Odd Functions</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Identify functions and their properties
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->


    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To activate prior knowledge of function graphs and symmetry, and to let learners conjecture the algebraic rules for even and odd functions from visual patterns before formal definitions are introduced.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Pose the anchor task below without revealing the terms <em>even</em> or <em>odd</em>. Circulate and ask probing questions. <q>What happens to the <m>y</m>-value when you replace <m>x</m> with <m>-x</m>?</q> and <q>Does the graph fold? Does it spin?</q>. Encourage learners to record observations in their own words before the class discussion.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the four functions below. For each one, complete the table of values for both <m>x</m> and <m>-x</m>, then sketch a rough graph on the axes provided.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p><m>f(x) = x^2</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>g(x) = x^3</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>h(x) = x^2 + x</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>k(x) = |x|</m></p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                After completing your tables and sketches, discuss the following questions with a partner:
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For which functions does <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m> for every value you tested? Describe the visual symmetry you notice on those graphs.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For which functions does <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m> for every value you tested? How would you describe the symmetry on those graphs?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Is there a function in the list that satisfies neither relationship? What does its graph look like?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <statement>
            <title>Exploring Operations on Even and Odd Functions</title>

            <p>
                Consider the functions <m>f(x)=x^2</m> and <m>g(x)=x^4</m>. These are even functions.
                Also consider the functions <m>p(x)=x</m> and <m>q(x)=x^3</m>. These are odd functions.
            </p>

            <p>
                Work with a partner. For each function below, find its value at <m>-x</m>. Use your results to decide whether the function is even, odd, or neither.
            </p>

            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Investigate the functions <m>x^2+x^4</m>, <m>x+x^3</m>, and <m>x^2+x</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        What do you notice when two even functions are added together?
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        What do you notice when two odd functions are added together?
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        What do you notice when an even function and an odd function are added together?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Investigate the functions <m>x^2x^4</m>, <m>x(x^3)</m>, and <m>x^2(x^3)</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        What patterns do you observe when even and odd functions are multiplied?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Investigate the functions <m>\dfrac{x^4}{x^2}</m>, <m>\dfrac{x^3}{x}</m>, and <m>\dfrac{x^2}{x}</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        What patterns do you observe when even and odd functions are divided?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Investigate the compositions <m>f(g(x))</m>, <m>p(q(x))</m>, and <m>f(p(x))</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        What do you notice about the composition of even and odd functions?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Discuss your results with the class and write a summary of the patterns you have discovered.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    20 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the concepts and properties learners discovered in the anchor task.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <p> 
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Ask two or three pairs to share what they noticed in the activity. Use their own language first (such as <q>the graph looks the same on both sides</q>) before introducing the terms <em>even</em> and <em>odd</em>. This makes the formal definition feel like a natural next step rather than something new.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>The Algebraic Test:</alert> Emphasise that the insight block sets out the single test learners need. Substitute <m>-x</m> into the function and simplify. If the result equals <m>f(x)</m>, the function is even. If the result equals <m>-f(x)</m>, the function is odd. If it equals neither, the function is neither. Stress that one counterexample is enough to rule out a classification.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Interactive Exploration:</alert> Direct learners to the JSXGraph interactive in the Exploration block. Ask them to drag point <m>P</m> on an even function and observe the horizontal dashed line, then switch to an odd function and notice how the dashed line always passes through the origin. This helps learners see the geometric meaning of the algebraic test.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <p>
            A function <m>f</m> is classified as even, odd, or neither by comparing <m>f(-x)</m> with <m>f(x)</m>
            for all <m>x</m> in the domain of <m>f</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            A function <m>f</m> is <term>even</term> if <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m> holds for every <m>x</m> in its domain.
        </p>
        <p>
            A function <m>f</m> is <term>odd</term> if <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m> holds for every <m>x</m> in its domain.
        </p>
        <p>
            A function that satisfies neither condition is called <term>neither even nor odd</term>.
        </p>
        <p>
            A polynomial function in which every term has an even power of <m>x</m> is an even function.
            For example, <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>g(x) = 4 - x^2</m> are even functions
            because every term involves an even power of <m>x</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            A polynomial function in which every term has an odd power of <m>x</m> is an odd function.
            For example, <m>p(x) = x^3</m> and <m>q(x) = x^5 - x</m> are odd functions
            because every term involves an odd power of <m>x</m>.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <exploration>
    <title>Even and Odd Functions</title>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-even-odd" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/even-odd-functions.js">
        <sbsgroup>
            <sidebyside width="95%" margins="2%">
                <slate xml:id="jsx-even-odd-functions" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
            </sidebyside>
            <sidebyside width="95%" margins="2%">
                <slate surface="html">
                    <div style="text-align:center;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
                        <input type="button" value="f(x) = x²" onclick="eoSelect(0);" onkeypress="eoSelect(0);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 8px;"/>
                        <input type="button" value="f(x) = x³" onclick="eoSelect(1);" onkeypress="eoSelect(1);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 8px;"/>
                        <input type="button" value="f(x) = |x|" onclick="eoSelect(2);" onkeypress="eoSelect(2);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 8px;"/>
                        <input type="button" value="f(x) = 2x" onclick="eoSelect(3);" onkeypress="eoSelect(3);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 8px;"/>
                        <input type="button" value="f(x) = x² + x" onclick="eoSelect(4);" onkeypress="eoSelect(4);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 8px;"/>
                    </div>
                </slate>
            </sidebyside>
        </sbsgroup>
        <instructions>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Choose a function rule using the buttons. Three graphs are drawn: the original <m>f(x)</m> (blue), its reflection in the <m>y</m>-axis <m>f(-x)</m> (green), and its rotation about the origin <m>-f(-x)</m> (purple).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            When the function is <alert>even</alert>, its reflection in the <m>y</m>-axis lies exactly on top of the original, so the green graph is highlighted. This shows <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            When the function is <alert>odd</alert>, its rotation about the origin lies on top of the original, so the purple graph is highlighted. This shows <m>-f(-x) = f(x)</m>, that is <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If neither comparison graph matches the original, the function is <alert>neither</alert> even nor odd. Check the classification shown at the top right.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</exploration>

    

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine whether the function <m>f(x) = -4x^2 + 7</m> is even, odd, or neither.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To classify the function, we evaluate <m>f(-x)</m> and compare it to the original function <m>f(x)</m>. We substitute <m>-x</m> into the equation:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>f(-x) = -4(-x)^2 + 7</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Squaring a negative value results in a positive value, so <m>(-x)^2 = x^2</m>. Substituting this back into the expression yields:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>f(-x) = -4x^2 + 7</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Because the resulting expression is identical to our original function, we have shown that <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>. Therefore, the function is even.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Classify the rational function <m>g(x) = \frac{3}{x^2 + 2}</m> as even, odd, or neither.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We test for symmetry by substituting <m>-x</m> into the function:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>g(-x) = \frac{3}{(-x)^2 + 2}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Applying the even exponent rule to the denominator, we know that <m>(-x)^2 = x^2</m>. The expression simplifies to:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>g(-x) = \frac{3}{x^2 + 2}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>g(-x) = g(x)</m>, the function is even.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine algebraically whether the polynomial function <m>h(x) = x^3 - 5x</m> is even, odd, or neither.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To test if a function is odd, we evaluate <m>h(-x)</m> and check if it equals <m>-h(x)</m>. First, substitute <m>-x</m> into the function:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>h(-x) = (-x)^3 - 5(-x)</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                A negative value raised to an odd power remains negative, so <m>(-x)^3 = -x^3</m>. Distributing the negative signs gives:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>h(-x) = -x^3 + 5x</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Next, we factor out a <m>-1</m> from the entire expression to compare it to our original function:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>h(-x) = -(x^3 - 5x)</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                The expression inside the parentheses is the original function <m>h(x)</m>. Because <m>h(-x) = -h(x)</m>, the function is odd.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show whether the rational function <m>k(x) = \frac{x^3}{2x^2 + 1}</m> is even, odd, or neither.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We begin by substituting <m>-x</m> for every instance of <m>x</m> in the function:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>k(-x) = \frac{(-x)^3}{2(-x)^2 + 1}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Simplify the numerator using the odd exponent rule (<m>(-x)^3 = -x^3</m>) and the denominator using the even exponent rule (<m>(-x)^2 = x^2</m>):
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>k(-x) = \frac{-x^3}{2x^2 + 1}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                We can factor the negative sign out to the front of the fraction:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>k(-x) = -\left(\frac{x^3}{2x^2 + 1}\right)</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since this matches the definition <m>k(-x) = -k(x)</m>, the function is odd.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-even-odd-functions">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine whether the following functions are even, odd, or neither.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x) = x^4 - 3x^2 + 2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>g(x) = 2x^5 - 7x</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>h(x) = x^3 + x^2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>k(x) = \frac{x}{x^2 + 1}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>p(x) = 5\cos(x) + x^2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>q(x) = 4x^3 - x + 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>r(x) = \frac{x^2 - 9}{2x^4 + 3}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>s(x) = x^7 + 3x^3 - x</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="3">
                        <li>
                            <p>Even</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Odd</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Neither</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Odd</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Even</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Neither</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Even</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Odd</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Study the graphs below. For each graph, identify whether the function it represents is even, odd, or neither.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="3">
                        <li>
                            <image xml:id="graph-exercise-even" width="100%">
                                <shortdescription>A parabola opening upwards, symmetric about the y-axis.</shortdescription>
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[below] {$x$};
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                                        \draw[thick, blue, domain=-2.2:2.2, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x - 1});
                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <image xml:id="graph-exercise-odd" width="100%">
                                <shortdescription>A cubic curve passing through the origin, symmetric about the origin.</shortdescription>
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[below] {$x$};
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-3) -- (0,3) node[left] {$y$};
                                        \draw[thick, red, domain=-1.4:1.4, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x});
                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <image xml:id="graph-exercise-neither" width="100%">
                                <shortdescription>A parabola opening upwards but shifted to the right, showing no symmetry with the y-axis or origin.</shortdescription>
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2,0) -- (4,0) node[below] {$x$};
                                        \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                                        \draw[thick, green!70!black, domain=-0.5:3.5, samples=50] plot (\x, {(\x-1.5)*(\x-1.5)});
                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="3">
                        <li>
                            <p>Even</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Odd</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Neither</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>Provide a structured two-column graphic organiser with the headings <q>Substitute <m>-x</m></q> and <q>Compare to <m>f(x)</m> and <m>-f(x)</m></q> so learners follow a step-by-step written routine before abstracting the rule.</line>
                        <line>Limit initial practice to polynomial functions with a single term (e.g. <m>f(x) = x^2</m>, <m>f(x) = x^3</m>) where the even/odd exponent rule is transparent, before moving to multi-term expressions.</line>
                        <line>During the interactive exploration, pair struggling learners with a peer and assign specific roles: one drags the point, the other records coordinates, so both engage actively without being overwhelmed.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>Ask learners to prove algebraically two or three of the properties listed in the insight block (e.g. prove that the product of two odd functions is even) using generic even function <m>f</m> and odd function <m>g</m> satisfying their respective definitions.</line>
                        <line>Pose the extension question: <q>Can a non-zero function be both even and odd simultaneously? If so, find an example. If not, prove it is impossible.</q> (Answer: only the zero function <m>f(x) = 0</m> satisfies both conditions.)</line>
                        <line>Invite learners to investigate whether trigonometric functions such as <m>\sin x</m> and <m>\cos x</m> are even, odd, or neither, connecting the algebraic test to known identities <m>\sin(-x) = -\sin x</m> and <m>\cos(-x) = \cos x</m>.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>


</section>
